<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shenglai Zeng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Michigan State University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qirui Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Xi’an Jiaotong University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kai Guo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Michigan State University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xinnan Dai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Michigan State University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xianxuan Long
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Michigan State University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hui Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Michigan State University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual Text Comprehension (VTC) renders text into images for a
vision–language model (VLM) to read, sidestepping LLM context-window
limits and powering applications from long-page OCR to multi-page memory
QA. Yet
existing VTC pipelines treat rendering and layout as a fixed,
content-agnostic preprocessing step, and offer little mechanistic
understanding of how VLMs internally process visualized text. Through a
focused empirical study on VTC QA tasks, we reveal that VLMs exhibit a
<em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime: evidence-localizing
attention emerges sharply in the middle-to-late layers and is largely
decoupled from answer correctness, yet simply enlarging the localized
spans on the rendered page recovers a large fraction of the failures.
Building on these observations, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR</span>
(<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ttention-<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>uided <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>daptive
<span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>endering), a training-free, model-agnostic method that
leverages a VLM’s own middle-to-late layer attention to identify the
top-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> important visual patches, maps them back to word spans, and
re-renders the page with those spans enlarged before re-inferring the
answer. Extensive experiments across nine VTC benchmarks (short-form,
long-context, and multi-page memory QA) and four VLM backbones show that
AGAR (i) consistently improves off-the-shelf VLMs as a plug-and-play
enhancement, (ii) composes with VLM post-training to yield further gains,
and (iii) remains robust under both visual- and text-side input
degradation.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The context window of large language models (LLMs) remains a fundamental
bottleneck that limits how much text a model can process in a single
pass. A recently emerging paradigm, <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Visual Text
Comprehension</em> (VTC) or <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optical context compression</em>, sidesteps this
bottleneck by <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rendering</em> text into images and letting a
vision–language model (VLM) read it directly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. A single visual patch can pack many
characters, yielding <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\!-\!20\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\!-\!20\times</annotation></semantics></math> token-level compression and
unlocking applications such as long-page
OCR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, long-context visual question
answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, vision-based long-term memory for
agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, reasoning-chain
compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, persona-conditioned
recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and long-context code
understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this momentum, current VTC pipelines leave two important gaps that remain underexplored.
First, most prior work treats rendering and layout as a fixed, content-agnostic step. But typography itself is a useful signal. Textbooks enlarge headings and bold keywords to direct a human reader’s eye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and similar visual emphasis could possibly route a VLM’s limited visual capacity toward task-relevant content.
Second, the community has limited <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mechanistic</em> understanding of how VLMs internally process VTC inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>: which layers actually localize task-relevant text, whether failures stem from attending to the wrong region or from mis-reading the right one, and how the model consumes the localized signal. Understanding these internal mechanisms is crucial for diagnosing bottlenecks and designing effective enhancement methods. By contrast, in adjacent areas such as evidence-based question answering and traditional VQA, prior work has shown that the internal representations and attention patterns of LLMs already contain rich, exploitable signals about what the model is truly attending to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, suggesting that a similar internal lens may be the missing tool for VTC as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the two gaps above, we conduct a comprehensive empirical
study on VTC QA tasks (§<a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) that examines how a VLM
reads long rendered text and where its failures come from, looking at
both its attention patterns and its answer quality. We make three key observations. <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, evidence-localizing
attention emerges sharply in the middle-to-late layers and is carried
by a substantial fraction of heads in those layers, consistently across
backbones.
<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, this localization is largely decoupled from correctness:
incorrect samples attend to the right evidence nearly as well as correct
ones, placing VLMs in a <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime.
<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third</span>, on failing samples, enlarging the ground-truth evidence
on the rendered page recovers a large fraction of errors with a high
fix-to-break ratio. These findings suggest that, at least to a large extent, the
bottleneck lies in <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utilizing</em> the localized evidence rather than
discovering it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the above observations, we develop <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR</span>
(<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ttention-<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>uided <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>daptive
<span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>endering), a training-free, model-agnostic wrapper around any
VLM that exposes layer-wise attentions. AGAR first leverages middle-to-late
layer attention scores to identify the top-<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> important visual patches
and maps them back to word spans; it then re-renders the page with those
spans enlarged and re-infers the answer. Extensive experiments across
nine VTC benchmarks spanning short-form, long-context, and multi-page
memory QA, and four VLM backbones (Qwen3-VL-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>,
InternVL3.5-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>,
GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and
Glyph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>), show that AGAR is
<span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span> training-free, raising VTC accuracy on off-the-shelf VLMs as
a plug-and-play enhancement; <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span> complementary to post-training,
yielding additional gains when its attention-guided magnification is
incorporated into supervised fine-tuning; and <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(iii)</span> robust to
both visual-side (lower resolution, font/style perturbations) and
text-side (distractor injection, noisy contexts) input degradation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify a <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime in
VTC: VLMs already attend to the right evidence in the middle-to-late
layers even when the answer is incorrect, yet enlarging those spans
on the rendered page recovers many failures, pinpointing evidence
<em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utilization</em> as a key bottleneck.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR</span>, an attention-guided adaptive rendering
method that closes this gap by using the model’s own attention to
select evidence spans and enlarging them in a re-rendered page, with
no weight or prompt changes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments across 9 benchmarks and 12 subtasks
on four VLM backbones show
that AGAR (a) improves off-the-shelf VLMs in a training-free manner,
(b) composes with VLM post-training to yield further gains, and (c)
is robust to both visual- and text-side input degradation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Visual Text Comprehension</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual Text Comprehension (VTC), or optical context compression, sidesteps
the LLM context-window bottleneck by rendering text into images and
letting a VLM read it directly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Building on this
paradigm, a growing body of work targets
downstream applications including vision-based long-term agent
memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, long reasoning-chain
compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, long-context code
understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, and generative
recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Most of these works, however, either
treat the rendering pipeline as a fixed, content-agnostic preprocessing
step, or train a dedicated model to pre-compress the context end-to-end
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>; a clear gap remains in understanding how VLMs
internally process VTC inputs and in exploiting such mechanistic signals
to drive context-aware rendering.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Utilization of (V)LLMs’ Internal Signals</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An emerging research direction focuses on utilizing (V)LLMs’ internal
signals to understand how they process input contexts. On the LLM side,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. [33]</a></cite> use hidden representations to identify
high-level concepts in RAG systems such as context helpfulness;
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [16]</a></cite> use last-token attention to surface important
evidence spans in text QA; and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. [34]</a></cite> exploit attention
scores to locate critical personalization signals in long user
histories. On the VLM side, prior work in classical VQA has shown that
attention-related signals can be used to amplify task-relevant visual
regions and suppress irrelevant ones, via Grad-CAM-style
attribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, attention-driven
masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and brightness/contrast
modulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. VTC, however, lies at the intersection
of these two regimes—visual inputs that encode dense, structured text
rather than natural-image scenes or pure-text tokens—and has not been
systematically studied through this internal-signal lens.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminary Studies</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before describing our method, we conduct a focused empirical study
across four open-weight VLMs (Qwen3-VL-8B, InternVL3.5-8B,
GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking, and Glyph<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Glyph is post-trained on
GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking for VTC.</span></span></span>) to understand <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> a VLM reads
long rendered text and where its failures come from. After
introducing notation and metrics (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), we
organize the study around three research questions that
progressively dissect the gap between <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">seeing</em> and <em id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">using</em>
evidence: <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span> (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 RQ1: In the VTC setting, where does evidence-localizing attention emerge inside a VLM? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) asks <em id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em>
evidence-localizing attention emerges inside a VLM;
<span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span> (§<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 RQ2: Are VTC failures explained by failed evidence localization? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) asks whether VTC failures are
themselves localization failures; <span id="S3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span> (§<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 RQ3: How can we help the model better utilize the evidence it already localizes? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)
asks how to help the model use the evidence it already localizes.
Each subsection ends with a boxed observation; together they motivate
the design of our method (§<a href="#S4" title="4 AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual Text Comprehension (VTC).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a long textual context <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rendered into one or more images
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{I_{1},\ldots,I_{|\mathcal{I}|}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{I_{1},\ldots,I_{|\mathcal{I}|}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a natural-language question <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, a
vision–language model (VLM) <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> produces an answer
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}=\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{I},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}=\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{I},q)</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that each ground-truth answer
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported by a set of <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence spans</em>
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subseteq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subseteq T</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the key evidence in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> supporting
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (provided as explicit annotations in the QA datasets we
use).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image patches and evidence labels.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The VLM’s visual encoder partitions <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> into a grid of patches and
emits <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> visual tokens <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, one per
patch.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              A ”patch” is the image region of one visual token (possibly multiple ViT sub-patches after spatial merging).</span></span></span>
Using the renderer’s word–bounding-box map together with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>,
each patch is tagged as <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence</em> or <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-evidence</em>:
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if patch <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> overlaps any rendered region of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>,
otherwise <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The evidence set is denoted as
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{E}}=\{p_{i}:y_{i}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{E}}=\{p_{i}:y_{i}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patch-level attention scores.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Attention is extracted from the last query token <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{last}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{last}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the
position that drives next-token prediction) to image patches. Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the number of decoder layers and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> the heads per layer. For
each layer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and head <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, the per-head attention vector is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}\;=\;\big[\,A^{(\ell,h)}_{q_{\text{last}},\,p_{1}},\,\ldots,\,A^{(\ell,h)}_{q_{\text{last}},\,p_{N}}\,\big]\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}\;=\;\big[\,A^{(\ell,h)}_{q_{\text{last}},\,p_{1}},\,\ldots,\,A^{(\ell,h)}_{q_{\text{last}},\,p_{N}}\,\big]\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(\ell,h)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(\ell,h)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the (post-softmax) self-attention map at layer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>,
head <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Two aggregated attention vectors are used downstream.
(i) The <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">head-mean attention vector</em> per layer,
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}^{(\ell)}=\tfrac{1}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}^{(\ell)}=\tfrac{1}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(ii) The <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">layer-range aggregated attention vector</em> over a contiguous
interval <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}=\{\ell:\lceil aL\rceil\leq\ell\leq\lfloor bL\rfloor\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}=\{\ell:\lceil aL\rceil\leq\ell\leq\lfloor bL\rfloor\}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a&lt;b\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a&lt;b\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_{[a,b]}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}|}\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}}\bar{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}^{(\ell)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_{[a,b]}\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}|}\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}_{[a,b]}}\bar{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}^{(\ell)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Localization quality (NDCG).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an attention score vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the binary evidence labels
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we measure its evidence-localization quality using Normalized
Discounted Cumulative Gain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Sorting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (NDCG) in descending order with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
denoting the patch at rank <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we define
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{DCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})=\sum_{r=1}^{N}y_{\pi(r)}/\log_{2}(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>DCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{DCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})=\sum_{r=1}^{N}y_{\pi(r)}/\log_{2}(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{NDCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})=\mathrm{DCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})/\mathrm{IDCG}(\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>NDCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>DCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>IDCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{NDCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})=\mathrm{DCG}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{y})/\mathrm{IDCG}(\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{IDCG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>IDCG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{IDCG}</annotation></semantics></math> is the DCG of the ideal ranking that places all
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{E}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{E}}|</annotation></semantics></math> positives first. NDCG <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a
perfect localization that ranks every evidence patch above every non-evidence
one. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A higher NDCG therefore means the attention scores <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> assign
larger weights to the patches that actually support the answer,
separating them from non-evidence ones.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12898v1/relative_attention_panel.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Relative attention (%) per layer on HotpotQA.
Red/blue lines: attention on Evidence/Non-Evidence tokens.
<math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>: correctly/incorrectly answered samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.12898v1/per_head_ndcg_heatmap_panel.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/116;" width="476" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Per-head NDCG of last-token attention to evidence tokens on
HotpotQA. Colors encode deviation from the per-model median NDCG.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>RQ1: In the VTC setting, where does evidence-localizing attention emerge inside a VLM?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> a VLM concentrates its visual attention when
reading rendered text, we measure two complementary signals on
HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> (results on other datasets are
deferred to App. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Layer-wise Evidence Attention on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>
and <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Per-head NDCG Heatmaps on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>): (i) the average <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative attention</em>
(%) on evidence vs. non-evidence image tokens at each decoder layer
(Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Localization quality (NDCG). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), where 100% denotes the
layer-wise mean attention so values above 100% indicate above-average
focus; and (ii) the per-head localization quality <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{NDCG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>NDCG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{NDCG}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, visualized as a
layer<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>head heatmap (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Localization quality (NDCG). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer-wise emergence of evidence focus.</span> Across all four model
backbones, the curves in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Localization quality (NDCG). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> share a
common pattern. In the early
layers (roughly the first <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/2</annotation></semantics></math>), the relative attention on evidence
and non-evidence tokens is statistically indistinguishable, both hovering
around the layer mean of 100%. Starting from the middle layers, evidence
attention rises sharply, opens a substantial gap above non-evidence
attention, and <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">remains elevated through the last layer</em>. The shift is
consistent regardless of architecture (Qwen, InternVL, GLM-4.1V) or
post-training (GLM vs. Glyph). A plausible explanation is that the
early layers are still performing generic visual-feature aggregation (e.g. character/glyph recognition over the rendered page), while the late-middle
layers begin task-conditioned reading: once the question token has had
sufficient depth to build up a query, the model selectively routes
additional attention to the spans that actually answer it. The same trend
also appears on other datasets
(Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Layer-wise Evidence Attention on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>,
Fig. <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix D Layer-wise Evidence Attention on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-head structure of localization.</span> Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Localization quality (NDCG). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that heads in the lower
half of the network have very limited localization ability, while the
upper half contains a population of heads whose NDCG sits well above
the median. Evidence localization is thus not a few isolated heads at random depths, but a <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">population</em> that activates together at middle-to-later layers. The same pattern appears on other datasets
(App. <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Per-head NDCG Heatmaps on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>,
Fig. <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix E Per-head NDCG Heatmaps on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these observations identify the middle-to-late layers as a
contiguous band where evidence-localization information is concentrated,
which helps us decide to use the localization signal from the upper
half of layers in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            See
Fig. <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix E Per-head NDCG Heatmaps on Additional Datasets ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> for per-head NDCG vs. head
rank and Table <a href="#A6.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix F Layer-range Aggregated NDCG ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> for layer-range aggregated
NDCG.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="78.79" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 78.79" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,78.79) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 75.33 C 0 77.24 1.55 78.79 3.46 78.79 L 473.92 78.79 C 475.83 78.79 477.38 77.24 477.38 75.33 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 75.33 C 0.69 76.86 1.93 78.1 3.46 78.1 L 473.92 78.1 C 475.45 78.1 476.68 76.86 476.68 75.33 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 10.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="58.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Observation 1.<span id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Evidence-focused attention emerges at middle-to-late layers, populated by
a band of evidence-attending heads, consistently across the four VLMs we
examine.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>RQ2: Are VTC failures explained by failed evidence localization?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural follow-up question is whether VTC failures are caused by
localization failures: when the model answers incorrectly, has it simply
failed to look at the right evidence? Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Localization quality (NDCG). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> splits the per-layer
relative attention curves by per-sample correctness
(SubEM <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1</annotation></semantics></math> vs. SubEM <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            SubEM is a proxy for evidence
use: if evidence text appears in the answer, it likely was used.
§<a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> adopts standard EM/F1.</span></span></span>); circles mark
correct samples, crosses mark incorrect. Strikingly, the two subsets share the same overall trajectory. Even on
incorrectly answered samples, the relative attention on evidence tokens
rises sharply in the middle-to-late layers and stays well above the
non-evidence baseline through the last layer. A modest gap between the Correct and Incorrect curves does exist, most
visible on the GLM-family models (GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking and Glyph),
where Incorrect samples sit slightly lower. Even so, on Incorrect samples the attention on evidence tokens
remains obviously higher than on non-evidence tokens. In other words, the model is already
<em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">looking at</em> the right evidence on a substantial fraction of the
samples it ultimately gets wrong. This points to a <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime: the model
correctly identifies which patches carry the evidence, yet does not always
translate that localized signal into the correct answer. Localization quality is therefore a necessary but not a sufficient
condition for VTC accuracy. Interventions that target the
<em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utilization</em> side, such as making the localized evidence easier
for the model to consume, have the potential to mitigate this gap (RQ3).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="81.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 81.21" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,81.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 77.75 C 0 79.66 1.55 81.21 3.46 81.21 L 473.92 81.21 C 475.83 81.21 477.38 79.66 477.38 77.75 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 77.75 C 0.69 79.28 1.93 80.52 3.46 80.52 L 473.92 80.52 C 475.45 80.52 476.68 79.28 476.68 77.75 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 12.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="60.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Observation 2.<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Localization quality is largely decoupled from answer correctness: incorrect
samples attend to the right evidence almost as well as correct ones. VLMs in
VTC are in a <em id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>RQ3: How can we help the model better utilize the evidence it already localizes?</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:46%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>SubEM transition matrix on HotpotQA (Qwen3-VL-8B, 1000 samples).</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S3.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Enlarged: T</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Enlarged: F</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Plain: T</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">26.0% (TT)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5.5% (TF)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Plain: F</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21.9% (FT)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.65pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">46.6% (FF)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the question raised by RQ2 (how to help the model better
<em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utilize</em> the evidence it already attends to), we draw
inspiration from how a human reader handles a long passage. Once the
salient sentences are spotted, a natural reflex is to <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">look
closer</em> at them, i.e., to magnify the relevant text. We ask whether the same intervention helps a VLM. To
isolate the effect from any
localization error, we use the ground-truth spans <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> as an oracle
and re-render each page with characters in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn at <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math>
the baseline font size, leaving the rest of the page (layout, surrounding
distractors, prompt, and model weights) unchanged. Re-querying Qwen3-VL-8B
on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> random HotpotQA samples
(Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 RQ3: How can we help the model better utilize the evidence it already localizes? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we observe that <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> of
originally incorrect samples are recovered
(<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{F}\!\rightarrow\!\mathrm{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{F}\!\rightarrow\!\mathrm{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) while only <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of correct ones
are broken (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}\!\rightarrow\!\mathrm{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}\!\rightarrow\!\mathrm{F}</annotation></semantics></math>), giving a fix:break
ratio of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 4{:}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 4{:}1</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>-point SubEM gain—all from
changing only the on-page typography. Magnifying the important
information thus has clear potential to help the model better utilize
the evidence it already localizes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="82.25" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 82.25" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,82.25) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 78.79 C 0 80.7 1.55 82.25 3.46 82.25 L 473.92 82.25 C 475.83 82.25 477.38 80.7 477.38 78.79 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 78.79 C 0.69 80.32 1.93 81.56 3.46 81.56 L 473.92 81.56 C 475.45 81.56 476.68 80.32 476.68 78.79 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 13.62)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="61.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Observation 3.<span id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Localized magnification substantially recovers VTC failures, indicating its
potential to mitigate the <em id="S3.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime
identified in Obs. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 RQ2: Are VTC failures explained by failed evidence localization? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three observations in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> suggest a simple but
effective recipe. Obs. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 RQ1: In the VTC setting, where does evidence-localizing attention emerge inside a VLM? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> indicates <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> to read the
model’s own localization signal: in the middle-to-late attention
layers. Obs. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 RQ2: Are VTC failures explained by failed evidence localization? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> suggests that this localization is, on its
own, often not enough to flip a failed sample. Obs. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 RQ3: How can we help the model better utilize the evidence it already localizes? ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> shows
that physically magnifying the localized text can recover a
substantial fraction of those failures. The
natural method is then to chain the three: <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">use the model’s own attention (especially the middle-to-late
part) to decide which words to magnify, re-render the page accordingly,
and re-query the model</em>. We call this method <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR</span>
(<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ttention-<span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>uided <span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>daptive
<span id="S4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>endering); a high-level sketch is given in
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Stage 1 – Attention-Based Evidence Localization. ‣ 4 AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. AGAR proceeds in two stages, all without modifying the model weights
or the prompt. We follow the notation of §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminary Studies ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> AGAR: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for VTC.</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">context </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, question </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, VLM </span><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, renderer
</span><math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, layer range </span><math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, top-</span><math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> size </span><math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, font scale
</span><math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{font}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">font</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{font}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{I}^{(0)},\mathcal{W}^{(0)})\leftarrow\mathcal{R}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒲</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{I}^{(0)},\mathcal{W}^{(0)})\leftarrow\mathcal{R}(T)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">

</span><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> baseline render with word boxes
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}\}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}\!\cdot\!\textsc{Forward}(\mathcal{I}^{(0)},q;\,\texttt{output\_attentions})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜶</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.072em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.072em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps" mathsize="0.900em">Forward</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.900em">output_attentions</mtext><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{(\ell,h)}\}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}\!\cdot\!\textsc{Forward}(\mathcal{I}^{(0)},q;\,\texttt{output\_attentions})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">

</span>
</div>
