<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">40.8</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+8.8%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">22.1</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+28.5%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">34.2</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+15.9%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">32.0</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+22.6%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">21.9</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+6.8%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">25.7</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+13.2%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">16.8</span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.9.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#006060;">(+38.8%)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.18.1.11.10" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.18.1.11.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5,384</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="64.61" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 64.61" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,64.61) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 61.15 C 0 63.06 1.55 64.61 3.46 64.61 L 473.92 64.61 C 475.83 64.61 477.38 63.06 477.38 61.15 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 61.15 C 0.69 62.67 1.93 63.91 3.46 63.91 L 473.92 63.91 C 475.45 63.91 476.68 62.67 476.68 61.15 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 12.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:3.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 41.86)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 1.<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> AGAR effectively improves VTC performance on off-the-shelf VLMs with
only a marginal increase in visual-token cost.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Composing AGAR with Post-Training</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many real-world VTC applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> adapt VLMs via post-training, raising
the question of whether AGAR’s gain persists on post-trained VLMs.
We test this on the short-form QA family (NQ / HotpotQA / NewsQA /
TQA) under two settings. <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(a) Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct vs. its
SFT counterpart</em> (Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Composing AGAR with Post-Training ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a)): we LoRA-fine-tune
Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct on <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mix4</em>, a balanced <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>k corpus sampled from the four training splits (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>k each, rendered at the
standard <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> px, no attention-aware augmentation), and evaluate on
the same held-out splits as §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Main Results: AGAR on Off-the-Shelf VLMs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(b)
GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking vs. Glyph</em> (Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Composing AGAR with Post-Training ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b)):
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Glyph</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> is a public third-party VTC
model derived from GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking via large-scale SFT+RL.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12898v1/sft_panel.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/126;" width="215" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>AGAR composes with post-training (short-form F1).
<span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> Qwen3-VL-8B vs. mix4 SFT.
<span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking vs. Glyph.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In both settings, AGAR is applied at inference only, with no change
to training data, objective, or weights. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Composing AGAR with Post-Training ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, AGAR continues to lift F1 on top
of every post-trained baseline across all four datasets in both
regimes. On some datasets, the further gain AGAR delivers over the
post-trained model is even comparable to, or exceeds, the gain
post-training itself delivered: on <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(a)</em> HotpotQA, Plain
VQA <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> SFT lifts F1 by <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.5</annotation></semantics></math> absolute, and SFT <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> SFT + AGAR
adds another <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+5.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+5.5</annotation></semantics></math>; on <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(b)</em> NQ, GLM-Thinking <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> Glyph lifts
F1 by <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.5</annotation></semantics></math> while Glyph <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> Glyph + AGAR adds another <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+8.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+8.5</annotation></semantics></math> on
top. AGAR therefore
composes effectively with VLM post-training to deliver a further free
inference-time boost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS3.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="64.61" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 64.61" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,64.61) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 61.15 C 0 63.06 1.55 64.61 3.46 64.61 L 473.92 64.61 C 475.83 64.61 477.38 63.06 477.38 61.15 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 61.15 C 0.69 62.67 1.93 63.91 3.46 63.91 L 473.92 63.91 C 475.45 63.91 476.68 62.67 476.68 61.15 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 12.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:3.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 41.86)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 2.<span id="S5.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> AGAR composes effectively with VLM post-training to further improve
performance.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Robustness to Imperfect Inputs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further ask whether AGAR remains useful under degraded
inputs—common in real-world VTC where pages are scanned,
compressed, or surrounded by retrieval noise. We probe two principal
degradation sources on Qwen3-VL-8B: <em id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i) image-level
perturbations</em> (low resolution, sensor noise, defocus blur) and
<em id="S5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii) text-side distractors</em> surrounding the gold evidence
(e.g., unrelated paragraphs from retrieval). Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4 Robustness to Imperfect Inputs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
reports plain F1, AGAR F1, and the gain <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\mathrm{F1_{enh}}-\mathrm{F1_{plain}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F1</mi><mi>enh</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F1</mi><mi>plain</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\mathrm{F1_{enh}}-\mathrm{F1_{plain}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12898v1/robustness_panel.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/125;" width="476" height="125"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR robustness on Qwen3-VL-8B / HotpotQA.</span> Blue:
Plain F1; orange: AGAR F1; box: absolute and relative <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S5.F5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)–(c)</span>: <span id="S5.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">visual</span> corruptions (downsampling,
Gaussian noise, blur). <span id="S5.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(d)</span>: <span id="S5.F5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">text-side</span> dilution
from L0 (gold only) to L4 (66 paragraphs, with hard negatives at
L2–L4).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual perturbations.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perturb the rendered HotpotQA pages (1000 samples) with three families of
image corruption applied directly to the PNG before inference: resolution
reduction (downsample by <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{25\%,50\%,75\%\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{25\%,50\%,75\%\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which simultaneously
shrinks the visual-token budget from <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="332" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>332</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">332</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{185,79,22\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>185</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{185,79,22\}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens),
additive Gaussian noise on pixel intensities (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\{0.2,0.4,0.6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\{0.2,0.4,0.6\}</annotation></semantics></math>
on the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> range), and Gaussian blur (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math> pixels);
plain vs. AGAR inference is then run on the perturbed images
(Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4 Robustness to Imperfect Inputs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (a)–(c)). The main observation is that AGAR is robust to visual degradation and
recovers a large fraction of the F1 that Plain VQA loses under it. Under
resolution reduction, for example, AGAR at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>79</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79</annotation></semantics></math> visual tokens (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math>
downsample) matches Plain VQA at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="185" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>185</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">185</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math> downsample) —
AGAR effectively buys back a full level of image quality / token budget.
Moreover, in some regimes AGAR’s recovery actually <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grows</em> with the
severity of the perturbation: blur <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> px yields
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{=}+17.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>17.6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{=}+17.6</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+65\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>65</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+65\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and Gaussian noise <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma{=}0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma{=}0.6</annotation></semantics></math> yields
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{=}+11.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>11.4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{=}+11.4</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+48\%</annotation></semantics></math>), against an unperturbed baseline of
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{=}+6.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{=}+6.8</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+16\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+16\%</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text-side irrelevance.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build a <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-level distractor-density ladder on HotpotQA (1000 samples
per level) mimicking a retrieval pipeline of increasing noise: L0 = <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
gold only; L1 = native HotpotQA distractor (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> gold <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>
distractors); L2–L4 add <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{8,24,56\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>56</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{8,24,56\}</annotation></semantics></math> hard negatives drawn from other
HotpotQA training questions. Gold paragraphs are always present but
randomly positioned. Plain F1 cliffs at L0<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>L1 (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="67.0\to 39.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>67.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>39.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">67.0\to 39.4</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-27.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>27.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-27.6</annotation></semantics></math>) and then drifts down only mildly (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to 30.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>30.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to 30.7</annotation></semantics></math> at L4). AGAR’s
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> stays positive throughout, with the relative gain at every
distractor level exceeding the clean-input (L0) baseline, and peaks
under moderate clutter (L1: <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+10.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+10.6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+27\%</annotation></semantics></math>; L2: <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+10.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+10.2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+29\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>29</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+29\%</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="64.61" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 64.61" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,64.61) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 61.15 C 0 63.06 1.55 64.61 3.46 64.61 L 473.92 64.61 C 475.83 64.61 477.38 63.06 477.38 61.15 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 61.15 C 0.69 62.67 1.93 63.91 3.46 63.91 L 473.92 63.91 C 475.45 63.91 476.68 62.67 476.68 61.15 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 12.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:3.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 41.86)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 3.<span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> AGAR is robust to both visual and text-side input degradation, and
effectively recovers much of the F1 loss caused by quality degradation.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Hyperparameter Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12898v1/sensitivity_2col_bar.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/124;" width="238" height="124"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR’s <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> landscape</span> on Qwen3-VL-8B on
HotpotQA and LB-QP. The red dashed plane marks Plain VQA.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer (iv), we study AGAR’s sensitivity to its two
hyperparameters: the fraction <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of patches to magnify and the
font scale <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the covered words. We sweep
<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{0.5\%,1\%,2\%,5\%,10\%\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{0.5\%,1\%,2\%,5\%,10\%\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{1.2,1.5,1.8,2.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{1.2,1.5,1.8,2.0\}</annotation></semantics></math> on all datasets from
§<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Main Results: AGAR on Off-the-Shelf VLMs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.5 Hyperparameter Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows
HotpotQA (single-page) and LB-QP (multi-page) as representatives;
the full sweep is in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Full (𝑘,𝛼) Sensitivity Sweep ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. AGAR is <em id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust</em> to <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>: nearly all cells exceed the
Plain VQA plane (HotpotQA: <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20/20</annotation></semantics></math>, LB-QP: <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19/20</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11/12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11/12</annotation></semantics></math> appendix
subtasks have <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 90\%</annotation></semantics></math> above-plane cells). Small <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>
underperform due to insufficient magnification against attention
dilution; larger values trade tokens for accuracy, tunable to budget.
Well-chosen pairs yield pronounced gains (HotpotQA F1
<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+14.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>14.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+14.3</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+35.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>35.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+35.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10\%,2.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10\%,2.0)</annotation></semantics></math>). Other
hyperparameters (base font size, layer range) are discussed in
App. <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Impact on Base Token Font ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> and <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Layer-range Aggregated NDCG ‣ Magnifying What Matters: Attention-Guided Adaptive Rendering for Visual Text Comprehension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="81.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 81.21" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,81.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A0A0A0;" fill="#A0A0A0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 77.75 C 0 79.66 1.55 81.21 3.46 81.21 L 473.92 81.21 C 475.83 81.21 477.38 79.66 477.38 77.75 L 477.38 3.46 C 477.38 1.55 475.83 0 473.92 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0F0;" fill="#F0F0F0" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 77.75 C 0.69 79.28 1.93 80.52 3.46 80.52 L 473.92 80.52 C 475.45 80.52 476.68 79.28 476.68 77.75 L 476.68 3.46 C 476.68 1.93 475.45 0.69 473.92 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.7 12.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.84em;--ltx-fo-height:4.23em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="60.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 58.46)" width="482.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.84em;">
<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 4.<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> AGAR is robust to its <math id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameters: most pairs already
improve over Plain VQA, while stronger <math id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> trade additional
visual tokens for substantially larger gains.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Limitation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We studied how VLMs internally process Visual Text Comprehension
(VTC) inputs and where their failures come from. VLMs reliably
localize evidence in middle-to-late layers, yet this localization is
decoupled from answer correctness, leaving them in a
<em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">localization-without-utilization</em> regime. Magnifying the
localized evidence already substantially repairs these failures.
Motivated by this, we propose <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AGAR</span>, a plug-and-play method
that uses the model’s own attention to enlarge identified evidence
patches and re-query the same VLM. Across four open-weight VLMs and
12 subtasks, AGAR consistently improves over Plain VQA,
outperforms training-free baselines, composes with post-training,
and stays robust under visual- and text-side degradation.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span> AGAR requires access to the backbone’s
attention scores, limiting its use on closed-source VLMs. Deeper
mechanistic accounts (e.g., circuit-level analysis) are left to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bib1" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">[1]</span>
